# rules: kotlin-cleartext-http-url
# languages: kotlin
# fixture: kotlin/CWE-319.kt
# from: CWE-319 (Cleartext Transmission of Sensitive Information)
#
# On a phone the network belongs to somebody else - a café access point, a
# captive portal, a carrier's transparent proxy - and every one of them can
# read a cleartext request and rewrite the response. That second half is the
# part people forget: this is not only a disclosure, it is a way to feed the
# app whatever the network decides to say.
#
# The rule reads the address at the point it is handed to something that
# connects, rather than every `http://` in the file. That is what keeps out the
# largest false positive in Android by a distance: XML namespaces.
# `http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android` is in every layout and every
# manifest and is not an address anybody dials, and a rule that grepped for the
# scheme would report it a hundred times per app and be turned off.
#
# Loopback and the emulator's alias for the host are excluded because neither
# is on a network anybody else is on. `localhost`, `127.0.0.1` and `10.0.2.2`
# are the three spellings that mean "this machine" from inside an Android
# process.
#
# What the counts say is worth writing down, because the number on its own
# looks bad. Over three real applications - now-in-android, Tivi and LeakCanary,
# about seventeen hundred files - this reports nothing at all. Over OkHttp it
# reports twenty-six, and every one of the twenty-six is in a test directory or
# in the documented samples: an HTTP library's test suite is about HTTP, and it
# dials cleartext on purpose. The rule is not wrong there and the corpus is not
# an application; a rule about addresses will always say most about the library
# whose subject is addresses.

["URL($U)", "URL($U, $$$_)", "Uri.parse($U)", "HttpUrl.parse($U)",
 "$B.url($U)", "$B.baseUrl($U)", "$B.setUrl($U)",
 "$V.loadUrl($U)", "$V.loadUrl($U, $$$_)", "$V.postUrl($U, $$$_)",
 "Request.Builder().url($U)", "Retrofit.Builder().baseUrl($U)"] as $dials
| "^\"http://" as $cleartext
| "^\"http://(?:localhost|127\\.0\\.0\\.1|10\\.0\\.2\\.2|0\\.0\\.0\\.0|\\[::1\\])[:/\"]" as $thisMachine
| scan_ast("*.kt"; $dials) as $all
| ( $all | of($dials)
    | where_capture("U"; $cleartext)
    | where_capture_not("U"; $thisMachine)
    | focus("U") )
| finding("kotlin-cleartext-http-url";
    "$U goes out in the clear, so whoever runs the network reads this request "
    + "and chooses the reply")
| report
